  "N7" [label="(7,0,0) High piezoelectricity is usually obtained by introducing structural instability, while high temperature stability prefers a stable structure."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes that Pb0.92Ba0.08[Zr0.50+xTi0.48-x(Nb0.5Sb0.5)0.02]O3 ceramics can be designed by simultaneously tuning the Zr/Ti ratio to approach a morphotropic phase boundary and using tape-casting, lamination, and hot-pressing to obtain a dense low-defect microstructure, thereby pursuing high piezoelectric performance together with improved temperature stability and thermal robustness.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Phase-boundary composition design and dense, low-defect ceramic processing target complementary limitations: the Zr/Ti ratio and morphotropic phase boundary address piezoelectric response, while tape-casting, lamination, and hot-pressing can be used as processing routes toward dense low-defect microstructures that support temperature stability and thermal robustness."];
  "N_Imp1" [label="(0,0,0) If developing piezoceramics with both properties is a cutting-edge task, then regulating the Zr/Ti ratio to approach the MPB is a method to promote these properties."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) The widespread use of piezoceramics together with the significant trade-off between piezoelectricity and temperature stability makes developing piezoceramics that address both properties a cutting-edge task."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because developing piezoceramics with both piezoelectricity and temperature stability is a cutting-edge task, regulating the Zr/Ti ratio to approach the morphotropic phase boundary is a proposed method to promote these properties."];
